WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.680
"Mortal Kombat Legends: Battle of the Realms" is a&nbsp;
study in contrasts. It has too much plot going on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:10.400
but it also has awesome action, ninjas,&nbsp;
and the voice of Joel McHale. Here are all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:14.160
the best and worst things about "Mortal&nbsp;
Kombat Legends: Battle of the Realms."

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:18.880
Johnny Cage is a "Mortal Kombat" mainstay&nbsp;
going back to the very first entry of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:24.080
hit game series. He brings to life a strong&nbsp;
hero archetype, bravado and a sense of humor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:28.320
as well. Looking at the discussions around&nbsp;
his absence in the recent live-action film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:31.360
and the fans' desires to see him&nbsp;
portrayed by the right actor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:35.120
it all shows how important he can&nbsp;
be to any project in the franchise.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.840
Joel McHale was a shining part of the previous&nbsp;
"MK" animated movie, "Scorpion's Revenge,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:44.000
and he continues that streak as one of the&nbsp;
best characters in "Battle of the Realms."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.760
His relationship with Sonya Blade&nbsp;
and the levity he offers is amusing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.240
without taking away from&nbsp;
what's going on around him.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:53.040
McHale has spoken about how&nbsp;
he improvised with his scenes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:56.400
and that may have helped make some of&nbsp;
his moments more genuine and funnier.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:00.800
The only complaint, of course, is that Cage&nbsp;
never secures that big win in this adventure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:04.880
but he's still a memorable part of the Earthrealm&nbsp;
defenders, even while being a bit of a jerk.

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:09.040
There are many different realms in the&nbsp;
"Mortal Kombat" lore — several smaller ones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:12.720
and six primary worlds, but it's&nbsp;
rare that any others than Earthrealm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:16.400
Outworld, and Netherrealm get to play&nbsp;
much of a part in the adventures.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:21.440
Even Edenia, where several characters hail from,&nbsp;
is usually relegated to a mention or the tiniest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:26.720
flashback. Worlds like Chaosrealm and Seido&nbsp;
are barely mentioned even in the newer games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:30.000
having become afterthoughts in the&nbsp;
character endings for the most part.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.000
This also means that smaller realms the&nbsp;
lore has introduced, like Vaeternus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.320
Zaterra, Arnyek, and Osh-Tekk, have&nbsp;
little chance of ever being relevant.

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:41.040
"Battle of the Realms" still has&nbsp;
a story that lives up to the name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:45.280
but it seems silly now to have hoped the film&nbsp;
would have seen our heroes go to these places&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:50.080
or even give us new information on them. Seeing&nbsp;
our champions jump across worlds for the various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:54.240
fights would have been quite the treat, but even&nbsp;
on the worlds that were used, there weren't many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.080
new details to be glimpsed. Even some of the&nbsp;
newer spots from the movie look as if they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:02.400
were taken directly from the games, such as&nbsp;
the shots of Shinnok's chambers, apparently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:07.840
directly inspired by the in-game cutscenes from&nbsp;
the Konquest mode of "Mortal Kombat: Armageddon."

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.400
"Scorpion's Revenge" gave fans a wonderful&nbsp;
look at the key parts of Shang Tsung's island,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:16.800
but the sequel film opened the battlefield&nbsp;
up a bit, offering animators a chance to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.240
show off some areas with which players&nbsp;
of the games have become quite familiar.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:24.640
The majority of "Battle of the Realms" takes&nbsp;
place in Outworld, but beginning portions show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:29.520
off a few locales on Earth, brief interludes in&nbsp;
Netherrealm, the meeting place of the Elder Gods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:33.600
and an incredible exercise in color&nbsp;
toward the end as realms begin to merge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:38.560
Most of these are gorgeous backgrounds and help&nbsp;
bring their individual parts of the story to life.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:42.480
Most notable on Earth is the Lin Kuei temple,&nbsp;
a site that has been shown in different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:46.720
forms before, but could never be explored&nbsp;
thoroughly enough. The audience sees a slow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:50.080
long shot of the main structure, the&nbsp;
outside courtyard, and a look at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:54.400
inner sanctum of the Grandmaster. Warm,&nbsp;
radiant, containing statues of famous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.640
Lin Kuei warriors - it all ties together&nbsp;
the foreboding feeling the scene presents.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:03.040
In Outworld, the participants express how&nbsp;
intimidating Shao Kahn's fortress is, and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:06.800
brief glimpse of his throne room shows off&nbsp;
that awesome green hue behind him — there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:10.640
is a lot of detail put into the arena&nbsp;
where most of the fighting takes place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:15.200
These are all solid representations of the&nbsp;
stages, mostly inspired by the newer games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:17.840
but the extra bits help make&nbsp;
the worlds feel more alive.

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:22.560
One of the awesome things that the last couple&nbsp;
of "Mortal Kombat" games did was progress the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:27.040
overall narrative, giving fans new events&nbsp;
and lore to chew on. With the subject matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:30.640
for "Battle of the Realms," there was hope&nbsp;
that the film would give us fresh tidbits of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:35.520
lore or clarify some of the existing meta-plot.&nbsp;
This isn't just to learn more about the realms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:38.880
but a hope that fans could see deeper&nbsp;
backgrounds for various characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:43.040
or some progression in the relationships they&nbsp;
share. None of this really happens, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:47.680
with the exception of Liu Kang, which is only a&nbsp;
brief moment that shows the death of his parents.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:51.920
What was that even connected to, and why were&nbsp;
those Tarkatans on Earth, hunting them down?

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:56.560
Those with a truly extensive knowledge of "Mortal&nbsp;
Kombat" could potentially fill in some gaps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:00.080
but not all of what fans tend to&nbsp;
believe is also considered to be canon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:04.720
so a film adaptation like this presents an&nbsp;
interesting opportunity to firm up aspects of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:09.120
the series' lore. The real missed prospect here is&nbsp;
not providing anything new about the Elder Gods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:13.920
the newer Titans, or especially The One&nbsp;
Being, who plays a pivotal role at the end.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:18.080
True, The One Being is supposed to be a&nbsp;
mysterious figure that no one truly comprehends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:22.320
but it seems like the writers purposefully guided&nbsp;
the plot around him — perhaps so they wouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:26.240
have to answer any questions or say something&nbsp;
new. It's a shame, and it feels like this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:31.520
promising alternate story, though full of passion,&nbsp;
falls short of doing anything truly spectacular.

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:35.920
Although the last one did a fair job in&nbsp;
delivering on violence and bloodshed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:38.400
"Battle of the Realms"&nbsp;
pushed it just a bit further.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:43.040
"Mortal Kombat" is a series that needs to embrace&nbsp;
its more vicious tendencies to feel at home;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:47.520
judging by the creativity in some of these&nbsp;
murders, they are loving the recent R-ratings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:52.240
Audiences will see some brutal finishes and a&nbsp;
couple of interesting takes on classic fatalities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:55.360
but a large portion of the carnage&nbsp;
comes from the standard fighting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:58.560
dispatching grunts in the most&nbsp;
over-the-top way possible.

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:02.960
Not only is there a ton of decapitations,&nbsp;
bone-breaking, and the ripping out of perfectly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:07.440
good body parts, but the first act takes&nbsp;
advantage of an army of disposable soldiers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:11.200
We see shots linger more when some of this&nbsp;
happens, and much of the art and animation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:16.080
choices around these attacks are meant to accent&nbsp;
the savagery each fighter possesses when pushed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:19.520
This leads to the use of more X-ray&nbsp;
moves that never overstay their welcome.

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:23.280
One of the problems with movies like&nbsp;
this is the desire to throw in as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:26.880
many extra characters as possible,&nbsp;
sometimes because it'll get the fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:30.240
excited to see one of their low-key&nbsp;
favorites — but more often than not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:34.160
they are in there because the script needed&nbsp;
someone to take a loss or eat a fatality.

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:37.840
For this installment, it seems like&nbsp;
Stryker and Jade took the bullet — both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:42.560
are introduced by name, get some lines, and&nbsp;
do absolutely nothing else worth mentioning.

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:46.880
Jade's relation to Kitana isn't brought up at&nbsp;
all, and she gets her skull cracked open by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:51.040
Sonya early on and then knocked off-screen&nbsp;
with an impressive uppercut from Liu Kang,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:53.360
never to be seen again. Stryker, at least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:58.000
gets a little bit more time to shine. He may&nbsp;
be "the new guy," but he's also a capable hero,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:01.920
and he at least receives a beautifully&nbsp;
disgusting death scene. Still, these are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:06.160
two great characters that feel wasted and would&nbsp;
have benefited from more moments focused on them.

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:11.120
Scorpion and Sub-Zero are two of the most iconic&nbsp;
characters in the "Mortal Kombat" franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:14.960
but players have a soft spot for all of the&nbsp;
various palette-swapped ninja characters from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:19.520
the games. Though "Scorpion's Revenge" gave&nbsp;
us the classic origin of the iconic ninja duo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:24.320
fans were excited to see the sequel bring&nbsp;
in Cyrax, Sektor, Smoke, and, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:28.560
the younger Sub-Zero, Kuai Liang, who's&nbsp;
seeking revenge for his dead brother.

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:32.480
These characters are all badass, and each&nbsp;
given at least a brief moment to shine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:35.360
Having their own storyline going&nbsp;
on outside of the tournament,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:40.000
but with their robotic arsenals and elemental&nbsp;
powers, their fights almost steal the show.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:44.800
That's right, "Battle of the Realms" crams the&nbsp;
Cyber Initiative story arc into a few spectacular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:49.280
scenes. Many of these characters fight together&nbsp;
and against each other, giving fans a lot to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:54.880
observe, especially when fighters like Scorpion&nbsp;
and Sub-Zero begin using their powers together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:58.000
But for many, the coolest bit is&nbsp;
getting to see Smoke in action,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:02.000
building up the friendship and respect he&nbsp;
has for Kuai Liang, and watching the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:06.880
unfold after his change. It's hard not to wish&nbsp;
for more of it, but their mission eventually had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:11.440
to feed into the bigger plot and take the focus&nbsp;
off of those incredibly cool ninja characters.

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:16.000
"Scorpion's Revenge" had its flaws, but&nbsp;
overall it pleased fans and gave them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:19.440
a reason to believe a sequel would deliver&nbsp;
the goods. While "Battle of the Realms"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:23.520
might be an adequate follow-up (especially&nbsp;
if only considering the first two acts),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:27.920
something about the ending just doesn't sit&nbsp;
right. It isn't horrible, but is nowhere near&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:32.640
the quality of the rest of the film. The third&nbsp;
act is much crazier — which is expected — but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:36.800
feels like it's from a different movie,&nbsp;
predominantly because of the two main fights.

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:39.760
The final battle against Shinnok&nbsp;
has a ton of moving parts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:44.720
and is on a much grander scale, with godlike&nbsp;
powers being thrown around. This makes sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:49.200
but it loses a lot of the intensity – not to&nbsp;
mention the blood – of the previous encounters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:54.320
even if the stakes are sky-high. No one can call&nbsp;
this anticlimactic; a little boring, perhaps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:58.240
but there should be some negative points awarded&nbsp;
for only having a slightly better version of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:02.080
ending from "Mortal Kombat: Annihilation,"&nbsp;
with the giant dragon and everything.

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:06.240
The fight between Liu Kang and Shao Kahn may&nbsp;
be worse, however. Some of the moves look like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:10.400
they were lifted directly from "Dragon Ball Z,"&nbsp;
but without the extra power levels to help it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:14.640
make sense. Both of these encounters needed to&nbsp;
stay a bit more grounded to stick the landing.

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:19.520
Many of the "Mortal Kombat" characters have found&nbsp;
their voices over the lifespan of the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:22.640
though the actors who portray them&nbsp;
often change. Even if what's coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:26.320
out of the combatants mouths might not&nbsp;
be a perfect recreation of recent games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:31.040
none of the voices in either of the two modern&nbsp;
animated movies have felt bad enough to stand out.

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:35.360
So, many of the talents who helped bring the cast&nbsp;
to life in "Scorpion's Revenge" returned to play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:39.600
the characters again, which is a testament to&nbsp;
how well they did — and a great number of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:44.000
voice actors fill their parts perfectly,&nbsp;
with many of them playing multiple roles.

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:47.280
It's always fun to hear words like&nbsp;
"Kamidogu" and "Outworld" uttered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:51.200
with such seriousness and precision.&nbsp;
New voices mix well with the old,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:55.040
forming a bloody chorus of grunts and&nbsp;
screams that work perfectly for the film.

00:08:55.760 --> 00:08:59.440
Animated features are typically shorter&nbsp;
than their live-action counterparts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:03.840
but "Battle of the Realms" clocks in at right&nbsp;
under 90 minutes. It's about nine minutes longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:08.000
than its predecessor — which again, tried&nbsp;
to cram too much into its plot — and here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:12.640
it's obvious that WB didn't learn from their&nbsp;
earlier mistake. The idea of not overstaying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:17.360
your welcome is certainly admirable, but why&nbsp;
throw so much into the mix if that's the plan?

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:20.000
There's enough plot here for&nbsp;
two feature-length projects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:24.000
making matters even more overstuffed than&nbsp;
"Scorpion's Revenge." The champions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:28.080
Earthrealm going to a "final" tournament in&nbsp;
Outworld feels like a plot that easily could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:32.720
have filled 90 minutes — but piled on top of&nbsp;
that is all the drama of the other stories too.

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:37.760
It'd be easy to go trim the fat, saving the&nbsp;
Kamidogu and The One Being for a follow-up tale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:42.080
when a true final boss was required.&nbsp;
However, screenwriter Jeremy Adams&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:45.920
has implied that the "Mortal Kombat&nbsp;
Legends" films were a two-chapter story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:49.360
meaning they had to put everything in&nbsp;
because there might not be others to come.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:54.640
If true, that is a huge shame, as doing so may&nbsp;
have hindered the overall story — and fans would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:58.400
have likely welcomed further animated&nbsp;
films in the Mortal Kombat universe.

